  {
    q: 'What is Al-Nadeeb and how does Qatar customs clearance work?',
    a: 'Qatar clearance runs through Al-Nadeeb, where the assessable math is a 5% duty on CIF before release. The importer or broker submits the import declaration through Al-Nadeeb, attaches the required documents, pays the assessed 5% duty on CIF, and receives release after any inspection or permit checks are completed.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What goods are restricted or prohibited in Qatar?',
    a: 'Qatar screens four high-risk categories strictly — alcohol, tobacco, pork and Islamic-sensitive goods — before adding narcotics, weapons, counterfeit goods, hazardous materials and certain animal/plant products to the controlled or prohibited list. Check the commodity against the General Authority of Customs before booking.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How does door-to-door shipping from China to Qatar work?',
    a: 'The door-to-door sequence wraps a 5% CIF duty and either a 22-day sea or 2–4-day air move into one forwarder-managed chain. The forwarder collects cargo from the supplier, handles China export clearance, moves it by sea or air to Hamad Port or Doha, files the Qatar import declaration, pays the 5% duty on CIF, and arranges last-mile trucking to the named address. Under DDP the seller or forwarder carries those costs; under EXW or DAP the buyer handles more of the import side.',
  },
];

const organization = {
  '@id': `${SITE_URL}/#organization`,
  name: 'WorldFreightHub',
  url: SITE_URL,
  logo: {
    '@type': 'ImageObject',
    url: `${SITE_URL}/favicon.svg`,
